</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="rxc-shell top" role="banner">
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3" y="3" width="12" height="12"/><rect x="9" y="9" width="12" height="12"/></svg>RadicalxChange</a>
      <span>/</span>
      <a href="/projects/">Projects</a>
      <span>/</span>
      <span class="here">Curtin on migration</span>
    </nav>
    <div class="right">
      <span class="status done"><i></i>Completed, October 2026</span>
      <a class="btn" href="mailto:info@radicalxchange.org?subject=Curtin%20migration%20results">Contact the team</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>


<header class="hero" id="top">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="hero-grid">
      <div class="hero-text">
        <h1><span class="ln">Curtin wants</span><span class="ln"><span class="box">a long-term plan</span></span><span class="ln">for migration</span></h1>
        <p class="sub">People disagree about how many should come, and how fast. But most want migration planned years ahead, with housing, roads, schools and hospitals keeping pace.</p>
        <div class="actions"><a class="btn solid" href="#groups">See what Curtin said</a><a class="btn line" href="#priorities">The changes people want most</a></div>
        <p class="turnout">8 to 27 October 2026 · 265 people took part</p>
      </div>
      <div class="cards" role="list" aria-label="Four statements from the conversation">
        <div class="card c1" role="listitem"><q>Immigration is fundamentally a good thing. But, for all concerned the rules should be clear. Decisions should be made promptly.</q></div>
        <div class="card c2" role="listitem"><q>Employers who exploit migrant workers should lose the right to sponsor visas.</q></div>
        <div class="card c3" role="listitem"><q>Migration keeps my workplace and my community going. Cutting it back would cost us more than it would save.</q></div>
        <div class="card c4" role="listitem"><q>For me, trust comes down to the numbers: too many people have arrived too quickly for our communities to keep up.</q></div>
        <div class="sticker" aria-hidden="true"><div><span>More than</span><b>3 in 4</b><span>in both groups want migration planned 10 to 15 years ahead</span></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<main>
<section class="glance" aria-label="At a glance">
  <div class="wrap glance-grid">
    <a class="gl" href="#groups">
      <h3>Two main groups</h3>
      <div class="split84" aria-hidden="true"><i style="width:84%;background:var(--g1)"></i><i style="width:16%;background:var(--g2)"></i></div>
      <div class="split84-n" aria-hidden="true"><span><i class="dot g1"></i>5 in 6</span><span>1 in 6<i class="dot g2"></i></span></div>
      <p>Five in six see migration working. One in six want fewer people, arriving more slowly.</p>
      <span class="more">See the groups</span>
    </a>
    <a class="gl dark" href="#agree">
      <h3>Both groups want a plan</h3>
      <p>Most people in both groups want migration planned years ahead, with housing, roads, schools and hospitals keeping pace.</p>
      <span class="more">See where they agree</span>
    </a>
    <a class="gl tinted" href="#priorities">
      <h3>First choice: a ten-year plan</h3>
      <p>At a workshop on 14 October, 65 of 72 people backed a ten-year migration plan reported to Parliament.</p>
      <span class="more">See the priorities</span>
    </a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="block" id="groups" aria-labelledby="groups-h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="groups-h">Most people fit into one of two groups</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="groups-row">
      <div class="gcard one">
        <div class="gtop"><span class="big">5<small> in </small>6</span><span class="who"><b>Group 1: Migration works</b>195 people</span></div>
        <div class="qbox lead"><p class="lab">What sets them apart</p><q>Migration keeps my workplace and my community going. Cutting it back would cost us more than it would save.</q>
          <div class="pcts"><span><i class="dot g1"></i><b>83%</b> of Group 1 agree</span><span><i class="dot g2"></i><b>7%</b> of Group 2</span></div></div>
        <div class="qbox"><q>Australians should not forget we are all migrants except for First Nations people as so deal with newer migrants humanely</q>
          <div class="pcts"><span><i class="dot g1"></i><b>92%</b> of Group 1 agree</span><span><i class="dot g2"></i><b>14%</b> of Group 2 <span class="nn">(14 voted)</span></span></div></div>
        <div class="qbox"><q>Someone who has lived, worked and paid tax here for years, and whose kids grew up here, deserves a real path to stay.</q>
          <div class="pcts"><span><i class="dot g1"></i><b>97%</b> of Group 1 agree</span><span><i class="dot g2"></i><b>47%</b> of Group 2</span></div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="gcard two">
        <div class="gtop"><span class="big">1<small> in </small>6</span><span class="who"><b>Group 2: Pace and control</b>37 people</span></div>
        <div class="qbox lead"><p class="lab">What sets them apart</p><q>A temporary visa should not lead to permanent residency.</q>
          <div class="pcts"><span><i class="dot g2"></i><b>94%</b> of Group 2 agree <span class="nn">(17 voted)</span></span><span><i class="dot g1"></i><b>16%</b> of Group 1</span></div></div>
        <div class="qbox"><q>For me, trust comes down to the numbers: too many people have arrived too quickly for our communities to keep up.</q>
          <div class="pcts"><span><i class="dot g2"></i><b>89%</b> of Group 2 agree</span><span><i class="dot g1"></i><b>26%</b> of Group 1</span></div></div>
        <div class="qbox"><q>We need net negative migration to ease housing and raise wages. Big business is funding mass migration and selling out Aussies</q>
          <div class="pcts"><span><i class="dot g2"></i><b>80%</b> of Group 2 agree <span class="nn">(15 voted)</span></span><span><i class="dot g1"></i><b>2%</b> of Group 1</span></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="small-note">The people who joined in aren’t a random sample, so these groups describe who took part, not all of Curtin. 33 people aren’t in a group: they voted on too few statements, or didn’t agree or disagree with any.</p>

    <div class="sub-head"><h3>Where everyone sits</h3></div>
    <div class="map-card" id="map-card">
      <p class="map-lead">Each dot is one person, placed by how they voted. People who voted alike sit close together, so the higher the pile, the more people voted that way.</p>
      <div class="map-top">
        <button class="someone" type="button" id="someone">Show me someone</button>
      </div>
      <svg id="map" viewBox="0 0 1000 560" role="img" aria-labelledby="map-desc"><desc id="map-desc">A map of 232 people. Group 1, 195 people, sits on the left, where people agreed that migration is working. Group 2, 37 people, sits on the right, where people agreed that fewer migrants should arrive more slowly. Where many people sit close together, the dots pile up. A dashed line inside Group 1 marks where its 77 people nearest Group 2 begin.</desc></svg>
      <div class="person" id="person" aria-live="polite"></div>
      <p class="map-hint"><span class="h-fine">Hover over a dot to see how that person voted.</span><span class="h-touch">Tap the map or press “Show me someone” to see how one person voted.</span></p>
      <div class="st-key">
        <p class="st-h">The two groups, and a closer look inside Group 1</p>
        <ul class="st-list">
          <li class="grp g1row"><p class="st-t"><i class="dot g1" aria-hidden="true"></i><span class="st-n">Group 1: Migration works</span><span class="st-c">195 people</span></p></li>
          <li class="part"><p class="st-t"><span class="st-b" aria-hidden="true">A</span><span class="st-n">Works</span><span class="st-c">118 people</span></p><p class="st-d">They see migration working. Few want permanent migration capped at what housing can take, and few say their community’s culture is changing faster than people can adjust.</p></li>
          <li class="part"><p class="st-t"><span class="st-b" aria-hidden="true">B</span><span class="st-n">Works, but slower</span><span class="st-c">77 people</span></p><p class="st-d">They see migration working too, and nearly all back a path to stay for long-term residents. But most want permanent migration capped at what housing can take, and say their community’s culture is changing faster than people can adjust.</p></li>
          <li class="grp"><p class="st-t"><i class="dot g2" aria-hidden="true"></i><span class="st-n">Group 2: Pace and control</span><span class="st-c">37 people</span></p><p class="st-d">They want fewer people, arriving more slowly, and temporary visas that don’t lead to permanent residency. There’s no clear split inside this group.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>


  </div>
</section>

<section class="block deep" id="agree" aria-labelledby="agree-h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="agree-h">Even where they disagree, they agree on something</h2>
      <p class="head-line">At least 7 in 10 people in each group agreed with 18 statements. Here are five of them, each beside a related statement the two groups disagree on.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="pairs"></div>
    <details class="all">
      <summary>See all 18 statements both groups agreed with</summary>
      <ol class="all-list" id="all"></ol>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="block" id="allies" aria-labelledby="allies-h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="allies-h">Unlikely allies on housing and living costs</h2>
      <p class="head-line">Beneath the main divide sits a quieter alliance: people in both groups who see migration through the squeeze on housing and living costs. They still disagree about how many people should come. But they agree that struggling with the cost of living makes people less welcoming. These are unlikely allies: people who usually disagree, joined by a concern most others don’t share.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="allies-grid">
      <figure class="map-card amap-card">
        <p class="map-lead amap-lead"><i class="dot ally"></i>Affordability allies</p>
        <svg id="amap" viewBox="0 0 1000 560" role="img" aria-label="The map of 232 people again, with the 52 affordability allies highlighted: 38 in Group 1 on the left and 14 in Group 2 on the right."></svg>
      </figure>
      <div class="aprofile">
        <div class="ablock">
          <h3>What they agree on</h3>
          <p class="vs-note">All 52 allies backed both statements. Here is how everyone else who voted on them answered.</p>
          <div class="acard">
            <p class="aquote"><q>People would be more welcoming of migrants if they weren’t struggling with the cost of living themselves.</q><span class="gloss">This takes people’s cost-of-living pressures seriously, without making migrants the problem.</span></p>
            <div class="vs" role="img" aria-label="Everyone else (87 voted): 45% agreed.">
              <div class="vs-row rest"><span class="vs-lab"><i class="dot faint"></i><span>Everyone else<span class="nn">87 voted</span></span></span><span class="vs-bar"><i style="width:45%"></i></span><b>45%</b></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="acard">
            <p class="aquote"><q>Universities that bring people here should have to make sure they have decent housing, not leave it to the rental market.</q><span class="gloss">This asks an institution that brings people in to carry the housing cost, rather than add to the pressure on renters.</span></p>
            <div class="vs" role="img" aria-label="Everyone else (87 voted): 37% agreed.">
              <div class="vs-row rest"><span class="vs-lab"><i class="dot faint"></i><span>Everyone else<span class="nn">87 voted</span></span></span><span class="vs-bar"><i style="width:37%"></i></span><b>37%</b></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="ablock">
          <h3>Why only 52?</h3>
          <p>Allies are people who backed both statements. Some people only got to vote on one of them, so the real alliance is probably bigger, perhaps around 80.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
