<section class="block" id="about" aria-labelledby="about-h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="head"><h2 id="about-h">How this worked</h2></div>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li class="step"><span class="fig">36</span><h3>Starter statements</h3><p>Kate Chaney’s team and RadicalxChange wrote the first statements to open the conversation.</p></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="fig">265</span><h3>People took part</h3><p>From 8 to 27 October they voted agree, disagree or pass, online or at a workshop on 14 October, and added statements of their own.</p></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="fig">2</span><h3>Groups emerged</h3><p>Polis, an open-source tool, grouped people who voted alike. Nobody chose a group. We named them.</p></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="fig">72</span><h3>Voters set priorities</h3><p>On 14 October they spread a budget of credits across 13 possible changes, using quadratic voting.</p></li>
    </ol>
    <details class="fine">
      <summary>Notes for checking the numbers</summary>
      <div class="arows">
        <div class="arow"><h3>Reading the percentages</h3><p>Each figure is the share of a group who agreed, out of those in the group who voted on that statement. Later statements had fewer voters, so where fewer than 20 people in a group voted, we show how many.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>Reading the map</h3><p>We rebuilt the map from the published votes using the same method as Polis, so it is close to the Polis report but not identical. Left to right is the main divide. Where many people sit close together, their dots pile up, so a dot can sit a little to one side of its exact place. How often each person agreed overall is on their card. Each person’s card shows up to two of their votes as examples. For each group they sided with, it shows the statement most of that group agreed with, preferring statements the person agreed with over ones they rejected.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>A closer look inside Group 1</h3><p>Inside Group 1, views change sharply near the border with Group 2. The 77 people nearest the border differ from the other 118 on 14 statements. On each, at least 65% agreed on one side and at most 35% on the other, with at least 10 people voting on each side, and a gap that big would happen by chance less than once in a thousand times.</p><p>Eight of them had at least 20 people voting on each side. A community’s culture changing faster than people can adjust: 76% of the 77 agreed, against 11% of the 118. Temporary visas staying temporary: 90% against 32%. Taking longer to get the checks right: 79% against 25%. Capping permanent migration at what housing can take: 71% against 16%. Keeping the visa appeals process from becoming a pathway to stay: 90% against 34%. English and an understanding of our values for family members: 81% against 29%. Putting people who can work ahead of family reunion when places are limited: 70% against 34%. Not choosing the best if most permanent visas go to people already here: 67% against 29%. Nearly everyone in both parts backs a real path to stay for long-term residents: 97% of the 77 and 97% of the 118.</p><p>When we shuffled the group’s order, no more than two statements ever split it this way. The 77 voted on a median of 40 statements and the 118 on 70, enough to place them. Inside Group 2 only one statement did, no more than chance, too few for a closer look.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>Affordability allies</h3><p>We looked for statements with similar support in both groups, then checked whether the same people backed them. For statements 33 and 29 they did: people who backed one had about ten times the odds of backing the other, even allowing for people who agree with most things. The link is clear in Group 1; Group 2 shows the same pattern, but it rests on 23 people. Of the others who backed one of the two, about half also voted on the other and didn’t back it. The other 38 didn’t get to vote on it. Had they voted like those who did, about 29 more would be allies, about 80 in all, but they may not have, and nearly all 38 are in Group 1. Treat this as a possible alliance, not a proven one.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>The group cards</h3><p>Each card shows three statements most of the group agreed with and far fewer of the other group did. RadicalxChange chose which three to show.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>Who and what we counted</h3><p>419 statements were open for voting: the 36 starter statements and 383 written by people taking part. The moderators held back 118 others, so no one else voted on them.</p><p>We count the 265 people who voted on at least one of those 419 statements. Between them they cast 21,315 votes. Polis’s own count of 266 also includes one account whose only vote was on a statement of its own that the moderators held back.</p><p>One person voted on enough statements to be placed in a group, but didn’t agree or disagree with any of them: every vote they cast was a pass. They count as taking part, but we haven’t placed them in a group, because a pass doesn’t show where someone stands. Polis places them anyway, because it reads a pass as halfway between agree and disagree.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>Pairing the statements</h3><p>RadicalxChange chose which statements to pair. Every number comes straight from the results. People who took part online and at the 14 October workshop gave very similar results.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>The priority vote</h3><p>A separate vote with its own voters. We set aside ballots that used fewer than 10 credits. Where someone voted more than once, we counted the last ballot they submitted. That leaves 72 voters. Net votes are votes for minus votes against. The matching statements from the conversation are close in meaning, not identical in wording.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>The data</h3><p>From the Polis export of 30 October 2026, after voting closed on 27 October. Statements appear exactly as people wrote them.</p></div>
      </div>
    </details>
    <div class="links"><a class="btn solid" href="https://pol.is/report/r9fyrr8m6yfmfbwscfvp2">Full Polis report</a><a class="btn line" href="https://pol.is/2mjb35sdvd">The Polis conversation</a></div>
  </div>
</section>
